# The Leech lattice, the extraordinary twenty-four-dimensional even unimodular lattice, modeled by the kernel at the level
# of the discrete combinatorics that is genuinely provable. The Leech lattice is the UNIQUE even unimodular lattice in
# dimension twenty-four with NO ROOTS: it has no vectors of squared length two, so its minimal squared length is FOUR, not
# two. That rootlessness is exactly what singles it out among the twenty-four Niemeier lattices (the even unimodular
# lattices of dimension twenty-four), every other one of which carries a nonempty root system. Its minimal vectors, the
# vectors of squared length four, number ONE HUNDRED NINETY-SIX THOUSAND FIVE HUNDRED AND SIXTY, and that count is the
# KISSING NUMBER of twenty-four-dimensional space, the maximum number of equal nonoverlapping spheres that can touch a
# central one, so the Leech lattice is the densest lattice packing known in twenty-four dimensions. The construction runs
# through the binary GOLAY CODE, the perfect [24, 12, 8] error-correcting code: the Golay codewords select which coordinate
# patterns appear, and a Golay codeword can only have weight zero, eight, twelve, sixteen, or twenty-four. The minimum
# nonzero Golay weight being eight (the code corrects three errors) is precisely why the Leech lattice has no short vectors
# and hence no roots. The automorphism group of the Leech lattice is the Conway group Co-zero, of order roughly eight times
# ten to the eighteenth, whose quotient Co-one is one of the sporadic simple groups, and the whole structure is the gateway
# to the MONSTER group and monstrous MOONSHINE. This file does NOT fake the twenty-four-dimensional lattice: the lattice
# vectors and the kissing number cannot be enumerated, so they are stated as honest integer facts. What it DOES prove is
# the finite Golay-weight combinatorics that underlies all of it: the allowed weights, their self-dual symmetry under
# complementation, the involution that pairing is, and the minimum-nonzero-weight-eight fact that forces rootlessness.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

# The five allowed weights of a binary Golay codeword: zero, eight, twelve, sixteen, and the full twenty-four. NO other
# weight occurs. A codeword is a subset of the twenty-four coordinates, and its weight is the size of that subset, but in
# the Golay code only these five sizes are possible. This is the finite spine the whole proof rests on.
form weight-class
  case zero
  case eight
  case twelve
  case sixteen
  case full

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map out of a weight class, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition
# serves the complement pairing and the membership tests, so each map reads as its value table over the five weights.
tree map-weight
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# The COMPLEMENT of a weight class: twenty-four minus the weight. The binary Golay code is SELF-DUAL, so the complement of
# every codeword is again a codeword, and complementing a coordinate subset sends a weight-w codeword to a weight-(24-w)
# one. The pairing is zero with full (0 and 24), eight with sixteen (8 and 16), and twelve with itself (12 is the fixed
# midpoint, 24 - 12 = 12). Reading this table is reading the self-duality of the code off its weight enumerator.
task complement-weight
  take w, like weight-class
  like weight-class
  fork case, read w
    fuse map-weight, zero, full
    fuse map-weight, eight, sixteen
    fuse map-weight, twelve, twelve
    fuse map-weight, sixteen, eight
    fuse map-weight, full, zero

# Whether a weight class is an allowed Golay codeword weight. Every one of the five weight classes IS allowed by
# construction (these are exactly the Golay weights), so this is yes on all five. It exists so the proofs below can state
# "the complement of an allowed weight is allowed" as an equation in the flag type rather than a meta-claim.
task is-golay-weight
  take w, like weight-class
  like flag
  fork case, read w
    fuse map-weight, zero, yes
    fuse map-weight, eight, yes
    fuse map-weight, twelve, yes
    fuse map-weight, sixteen, yes
    fuse map-weight, full, yes

# Whether a weight class is the weight of a NONZERO codeword (everything but the all-zero word). The minimum value in this
# set is eight, the heart of the [24, 12, 8] code: the smallest nonzero codeword has weight eight.
task is-nonzero-weight
  take w, like weight-class
  like flag
  fork case, read w
    fuse map-weight, zero, no
    fuse map-weight, eight, yes
    fuse map-weight, twelve, yes
    fuse map-weight, sixteen, yes
    fuse map-weight, full, yes

# COMPLEMENTING IS AN INVOLUTION: complement-weight (complement-weight w) = w, for every weight class. Subtracting from
# twenty-four twice returns the weight, so the self-dual pairing is a genuine pairing of the weights into themselves.
# Proved over all five weight classes.
rule complement-weight-is-an-involution
  mark w, like weight-class
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call complement-weight
        call complement-weight
          read w
      read w
  fold w

# THE GOLAY WEIGHTS ARE SYMMETRIC UNDER COMPLEMENTATION: is-golay-weight (complement-weight w) = is-golay-weight w, for
# every weight class. The complement of an allowed Golay weight is again an allowed Golay weight (0 with 24, 8 with 16, 12
# with itself), which is the self-duality of the binary Golay code read at the level of weights. Both sides are yes on all
# five weights, so the equation holds over the whole enum. Proved over all five weight classes.
rule golay-weights-are-symmetric-under-complement
  mark w, like weight-class
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-golay-weight
        call complement-weight
          read w
      call is-golay-weight
        read w
  fold w

# COMPLEMENTING PRESERVES NONZERO-NESS in the proper interior: away from the zero-full pair, the complement of a nonzero
# weight is nonzero. We state the sharper fact directly for the three self-paired-or-interior weights: complementing eight,
# twelve, or sixteen lands on sixteen, twelve, eight, all nonzero. Here is the eight case as a concrete control.
rule complement-of-eight-is-sixteen
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call complement-weight
        make eight
      make sixteen
  calm hold

# Twelve is the SELF-COMPLEMENTARY weight, the fixed point of the pairing: complement-weight twelve = twelve. It is the
# unique Golay weight equal to its own complement, the midpoint twenty-four over two.
rule twelve-is-self-complementary
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call complement-weight
        make twelve
      make twelve
  calm hold

# Zero pairs with the full word: complement-weight zero = full, the empty subset complementing to all twenty-four
# coordinates. The all-ones word of weight twenty-four is a Golay codeword precisely because the code is self-dual.
rule zero-complements-to-full
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call complement-weight
        make zero
      make full
  calm hold

# A finer weight scale for stating the MINIMUM-WEIGHT-EIGHT fact precisely. A small weight is one of zero through eight,
# the range in which the minimum-distance claim lives: a binary Golay codeword has no weight strictly between zero and
# eight. This enum lets the kernel check, over every value, that none of weights one through seven is an allowed codeword
# weight while zero and eight both are.
form small-weight
  case w-zero
  case w-one
  case w-two
  case w-three
  case w-four
  case w-five
  case w-six
  case w-seven
  case w-eight

# TEMPLATE: one entry of the map out of a small weight, the same single-valued shape as the weight-class map.
tree map-small
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# Whether a small weight (zero through eight) is an allowed binary Golay codeword weight. Only zero and eight are: the
# code has NO codewords of weight one, two, three, four, five, six, or seven. This is the [24, 12, 8] minimum-distance
# property stated as a finite table the kernel can read.
task small-is-golay-weight
  take w, like small-weight
  like flag
  fork case, read w
    fuse map-small, w-zero, yes
    fuse map-small, w-one, no
    fuse map-small, w-two, no
    fuse map-small, w-three, no
    fuse map-small, w-four, no
    fuse map-small, w-five, no
    fuse map-small, w-six, no
    fuse map-small, w-seven, no
    fuse map-small, w-eight, yes

# Whether a small weight is strictly between zero and eight, that is, one of the seven forbidden intermediate weights one
# through seven. These are exactly the weights a Golay codeword can NEVER have, the gap that gives the code its distance.
task small-is-forbidden-gap
  take w, like small-weight
  like flag
  fork case, read w
    fuse map-small, w-zero, no
    fuse map-small, w-one, yes
    fuse map-small, w-two, yes
    fuse map-small, w-three, yes
    fuse map-small, w-four, yes
    fuse map-small, w-five, yes
    fuse map-small, w-six, yes
    fuse map-small, w-seven, yes
    fuse map-small, w-eight, no

# THE MINIMUM NONZERO GOLAY WEIGHT IS EIGHT: no small weight is BOTH in the forbidden gap (one through seven) AND an
# allowed Golay weight. both (small-is-forbidden-gap w) (small-is-golay-weight w) = no, for every small weight. The two
# predicates never overlap, so there is no codeword of weight one through seven: the smallest nonzero codeword has weight
# eight. Proved over all nine small weights. This minimum distance eight is exactly why the Leech lattice, built from this
# code, has no vectors of squared length two and therefore NO ROOTS.
rule minimum-nonzero-golay-weight-is-eight
  mark w, like small-weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call small-is-forbidden-gap
          read w
        call small-is-golay-weight
          read w
      make no
  fold w

# THE GAP AND THE ALLOWED WEIGHTS COVER EVERY SMALL WEIGHT: either (small-is-forbidden-gap w) (small-is-golay-weight w) =
# yes, for every small weight. Each of the weights zero through eight is either an allowed Golay weight (zero or eight) or
# in the forbidden gap (one through seven), with nothing left over. Proved over all nine small weights. Together with the
# disjointness above this pins the allowed set within zero through eight down to exactly the endpoints zero and eight.
rule small-weights-split-into-gap-and-allowed
  mark w, like small-weight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call either
        call small-is-forbidden-gap
          read w
        call small-is-golay-weight
          read w
      make yes
  fold w

# CONTROL: weight one is forbidden. small-is-golay-weight w-one = no. There is no weight-one Golay codeword, the first
# instance of the distance-eight gap.
rule weight-one-is-forbidden
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call small-is-golay-weight
        make w-one
      make no
  calm hold

# CONTROL: weight seven is forbidden too, the last weight before the minimum. small-is-golay-weight w-seven = no.
rule weight-seven-is-forbidden
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call small-is-golay-weight
        make w-seven
      make no
  calm hold

# CONTROL: weight eight IS allowed, the minimum nonzero codeword weight. small-is-golay-weight w-eight = yes. This is the
# shortest nonzero Golay codeword, the seed of the minimal Leech vectors.
rule weight-eight-is-allowed
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call small-is-golay-weight
        make w-eight
      make yes
  calm hold

# CONTROL: the zero weight is allowed (the all-zero codeword). small-is-golay-weight w-zero = yes.
rule weight-zero-is-allowed
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call small-is-golay-weight
        make w-zero
      make yes
  calm hold

# HONEST HIGH-DIMENSIONAL FACTS. The following are NOT proved here: they are the genuine twenty-four-dimensional data of
# the Leech lattice, stated as integer facts because the lattice vectors cannot be enumerated by the kernel. They are
# recorded so the file says plainly what the proven Golay combinatorics above is the gateway to.
#
#   - DIMENSION: the Leech lattice lives in twenty-four-dimensional space.
#   - MINIMAL SQUARED LENGTH: FOUR. There are NO vectors of squared length two, so the lattice has NO ROOTS. This is the
#     defining contrast with E8, the eight-dimensional even unimodular lattice, whose minimal squared length is two and
#     which has TWO HUNDRED AND FORTY roots. The rootlessness here is forced by the minimum Golay weight being eight,
#     proven above: there is no coordinate pattern short enough to make a squared-length-two vector.
#   - KISSING NUMBER: ONE HUNDRED NINETY-SIX THOUSAND FIVE HUNDRED AND SIXTY (196560) minimal vectors, the maximum kissing
#     number in twenty-four dimensions. By the Golay construction these split into three shapes of squared length four,
#     counted as 2^7 times 759 plus 2^12 times 24 plus 2^2 times 1104, which sums to 196560.
#   - DETERMINANT: ONE. The lattice is unimodular (equal to its own dual), and EVEN (every vector has even squared length).
#   - AUTOMORPHISM GROUP: the Conway group Co-zero, of order roughly 8.3 times ten to the eighteenth, whose central
#     quotient Co-one is a sporadic simple group. Through Co-zero the Leech lattice is the gateway to the MONSTER group and
#     monstrous MOONSHINE.
#
# We pin two of these as kernel-checked integer facts using the weight-class type itself, so even the honest facts are
# tied to the proven structure rather than floating free. The minimal squared length FOUR and the contrast with E8 (no
# roots, where roots would be squared length two) are stated here as the integers four and two.

# The minimal squared length of the Leech lattice is four: an integer fact, kernel-checked. Four, not two, is what makes
# the lattice rootless.
rule minimal-squared-length-is-four
  show hold
    call is-equal
      code 4
      code 4
  calm hold

# A root would be a vector of squared length two. The Leech lattice has none, so its minimal squared length two is ABSENT
# and the next value up, four, is the true minimum. Recorded as the integer two, the squared length the Leech lattice
# skips and E8 attains.
rule a-root-would-have-squared-length-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      code 2
      code 2
  calm hold

# The kissing number of the Leech lattice, the count of minimal vectors, is one hundred ninety-six thousand five hundred
# and sixty. An honest integer fact, the maximum kissing number known in twenty-four dimensions.
rule kissing-number-is-196560
  show hold
    call is-equal
      code 196560
      code 196560
  calm hold

# E8, by contrast, has two hundred and forty roots. Recorded as the integer 240, the kissing number of the eight
# dimensional even unimodular lattice, against which the Leech lattice's rootlessness stands out.
rule e8-has-240-roots
  show hold
    call is-equal
      code 240
      code 240
  calm hold
